# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnpm-store
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# package build output. `packages/*/src` is generated but committed, because
# `build-react.mjs --check` diffs against it in CI; `dist` is compiled from
# that source at publish time and would only be a second copy in the history.
packages/*/dist

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# local, machine-specific agent settings
.claude/settings.local.json

# Agent skills are tracked. The drawing workflow under `.claude/skills/` is
# what AGENTS.md sends every agent to read before it touches `raw/`, and a
# gitignored path cannot back that instruction: every other checkout would
# find a dead reference. The directory used to be ignored on the theory that
# the method stays private while the icons ship; reversed on 8 Sept 2026, and
# the skills are repository content now.
#
# `.claude/settings.local.json` above stays ignored: machine-specific state.
# `.claude/launch.json` stays tracked: a dev-server config, not knowledge.

# The carousel sheets for the Figma Community listing, rasterised by
# `build-community.mjs` from `icons/` at 1920x1080.
#
# Ignored because they are output, not source: six PNGs of a full-bleed grid,
# regenerated by `pnpm community:build` whenever the listing needs them. The
# thing worth versioning is the file that composes them, which is committed.
#
# This is not the exemption it used to be. The first set really was exported by
# hand from Figma frames and could not be rebuilt, which is how one of them
# reached the listing with no icons on it. `community:check` now runs in CI and
# fails on any drawing a sheet names that `icons/` no longer has, so a rename
# is caught here rather than on the Community page.
previews/community/

# The sharp treatment's working record: the prototype history, the handover
# documents, the one-shot scripts, the staged geometry and the drawings handed
# over by hand.
#
# Ignored because this is how the work was done,
# not what the work produced. A handover is written from one session to the
# next and reads like it, and a public repository is not where an internal note
# about what is unfinished and who has to decide it belongs. The drawings it
# was staging now live in `raw/`, which is the source this project does give
# away, so nothing here is load-bearing for anyone outside it.
#
# The whole directory rather than a list of names, for the reason the
# block below gives: a document written tomorrow is private by default and
# cannot be published by forgetting a line here.
previews/sharp-history/

# Announcement banners, and the copy that goes out with them.
#
# `marketing/` is its own git repository pointed at a private remote, and this
# line is what keeps the two from seeing each other. How a release gets
# announced is not part of what this project gives away: the templates, the
# draft posts and the rendered PNGs are working material, and a public repo is
# a strange place to leave a half-written tweet.
#
# The whole directory rather than a list of names, for the reason the
# sharp-history block above gives: something added tomorrow is private by
# default and cannot be published by forgetting a line here.
#
# The generator in there reads `icons/` and `lib/icon-history.json` out of this
# repository, so a banner cannot claim a version or a drawing the set does not
# have. That dependency is deliberate and one-way. Nothing here reads anything
# in `marketing/`, and `pnpm icons:ci` passes with the directory deleted.
marketing/
